"Empty search results\nNo results were found for the provided queries--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.00341v1] Reversal Invariance in Autoregressive Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.00341v1)\nciteturn2065view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.00341v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 175\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:  cite57†Image arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! cite1†Learn more†info.arxiv.org ×\nL3: \nL4: cite2†  cite3† Search  cite4†Submit cite5†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite6†Log in L5: \nL6: Search arXiv\nL7: \nL8: Press Enter to search · cite7†Advanced search L9: \nL10: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL11: \nL12: arXiv:2511.00341v1 (cs)\nL13: \nL14: [Submitted on 1 Nov 2025]\nL15: # Title:Reversal Invariance in Autoregressive Language Models\nL16: \nL17: Authors:cite8†Mihir Sahasrabudhe L18: \nL19: View a PDF of the paper titled Reversal Invariance in Autoregressive Language Models, by Mihir Sahasrabudhe\nL20: \nL21: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L22: > Abstract:We formalize a structural property of the causal (autoregressive) language modeling (CLM) objective: reversal invariance. Formally, the next-token prediction loss assigns identical likelihood to a corpus and its reversal, implying that standard CLM pretraining is direction-blind. This symmetry explains why models trained on reversed text can achieve comparable performance to those trained on forward text, despite the inherently time-asymmetric nature of human language and reasoning.\nL23: We argue that this invariance represents a limitation of current pretraining objectives rather than a benign artifact. If natural language encodes directional dependencies - phonological, morphological, or causal - a symmetric objective may fail to capture them. We therefore propose viewing pretraining through the lens of temporal asymmetry, motivating future work on loss functions and architectures that explicitly model the arrow of language while retaining standard language modeling capacity.\nL24: Comments: 7 pages, theoretical note\nL25: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL26: Cite as: cite11†arXiv:2511.00341 [cs.CL]\nL27:   (or  cite12†arXiv:2511.00341v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL28:   cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.00341†doi.org L29: \nL30: Focus to learn more\nL31: \nL32:   arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL33: ## Submission history\nL34: \nL35: From: Mihir Sahasrabudhe [cite14†view email ]\nL36: [v1] Sat, 1 Nov 2025 00:51:46 UTC (16 KB)\nL37: \nL38: Full-text links:\nL39: \nL40: ## Access Paper:\nL41: \nL42:  View a PDF of the paper titled Reversal Invariance in Autoregressive Language Models, by Mihir Sahasrabudhe\nL43: \nL44:   * cite9†View PDF L45:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L46:   * cite15†TeX Source  L47: \nL48: cite16† view license †creativecommons.org L49: ### Current browse context:\nL50: \nL51: cs.CL\nL52: \nL53: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L54: \nL55: cite19†new |  cite20†recent | cite21†2025-11 L56: \nL57: Change to browse by:\nL58: \nL59: cite22†cs L60: \nL61: ### References & Citations\nL62: \nL63:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L64:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L65:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L66: \nL67: export BibTeX citation Loading...\nL68: \nL69: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL70: \nL71: ×\nL72: \nL73: loading...\nL74: \nL75: Data provided by:\nL76: \nL77: ### Bookmark\nL78: \nL79: cite26† †www.bibsonomy.org cite27† †reddit.com L80: \nL81: Bibliographic Tools\nL82: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL83: \nL84: Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL85: \nL86: Bibliographic Explorer (cite28†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL87: \nL88: Connected Papers Toggle\nL89: \nL90: Connected Papers 